// expect-exit: 94
//
// `Vec<T>` — OUT OF RANGE: `vec_init_zeroed` with a NEGATIVE length. RX-139.
//
// **FOUND BY THE AUDIT TRIAGE AND NOT BY THE AUDIT**, while establishing the
// full extent of the dead-`Vec` finding rather than fixing it where it was
// reported. It is the worse of the two: the others hang or trap, and this one
// **exited 0 having written outside its block**.
//
// `vec_init_zeroed(-5)` floored the ALLOCATION to one element and wrote `-5`
// straight into `count`. `vec_push`'s guard is `v.count >= v.cap`, which read
// `-5 >= 1` and was FALSE, so the push went to `items[-5]` — five elements
// BEFORE the block — and the program exited 0. Silently, and at the one
// constructor in this file that could produce an invalid `Vec` without a caller
// building a struct literal by hand.
//
// Every other entry point in `vec.npk` already trapped on a negative:
// `vec_reserve` on `need`, `vec_truncate` on `n`, both accessors on `i`.
// The half of the sweep that was missing was the one where the negative goes
// into a FIELD rather than into an index.
//
// ONE CASE PER FILE, because a trapping call cannot be followed by an assertion.
mod:vec_oob_init_zeroed_negative;

use "../../src/core/vec.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:argv) {
    // -5 with no arguments, and derived so the optimiser cannot fold it: a
    // constant would let `opt -O2` decide the whole program at compile time and
    // this file has to run at both -O0 and -O2 (rule B-3).
    int64:neg = 0i64 - argv.len - 4i64;

    Vec<int64>:v = raw vec_init_zeroed::<int64>(neg);

    // Unreachable. Reaching it means the constructor accepted a negative count,
    // and the push below is the write that used to land before the block.
    drop vec_push(@v, 7i64);
    drop vec_free(@v);
    exit 50i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
